From 1f97a523ba379d2c895d631f95358aa8639f9d35 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bjorn Pagen <11238136+bjornpagen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 21:20:02 -0600
Subject: [PATCH v1 4/4] pg_plan_advice: Add relation-qualified identifiers.

Relation identifiers are unique within a query, but advice is not applied
to one query: pg_plan_advice.advice is in force for every query planned
in its scope, whether set for a session, a role, a transaction or a
function, and that includes queries the user never wrote.  The queries
ri_triggers.c runs to check foreign keys alias every table "x", and
ORM-generated SQL repeats aliases across statements the same way.  So an
item meant for one query's relation lands on another query's relation of
the same alias, reporting "matched, inapplicable, failed" there, and two
items meant for two such relations conflict in both queries, so neither
is enforced.  Per-table advice could not reach the foreign-key checks at
all.

Let an identifier name the relation its alias must refer to:

    alias#occurrence:schema.relation/partition_schema.partition@plan

A qualified identifier matches what the unqualified one would, provided
the alias refers to that relation (for a partition, the partitioned
table, the partition itself still being named after the slash), and
nothing otherwise.  The relation must be schema-qualified, as a partition
must.  The qualifier only narrows a match, so identifiers stay unique,
and generated advice, which identifies relations within the query it
came from, never includes it.  With

    INDEX_SCAN(x:public.ref public.ref_pkey)
    INDEX_SCAN(x:public.tgt public.tgt_pkey)

in force, deleting a key from tgt checks both tables with their keys;
measured on a referencing table of 60,000 rows that statistics say is
empty, the "is the deleted key still referenced?" check reads 2 buffers
instead of 12,425.

The partition component could not simply be generalized to name the
relation an RTE scans: under traditional inheritance the parent's own
rows are scanned by a child RTE with the parent's alias and relation,
already identified as parent/schema.parent, which would then match two
RTEs.

The three places that compared identifiers field by field now share one
function, pgpa_identifier_matches().

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
